<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Innsbruck</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Innsbruck is the capital of the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, on the Inn at the foot of the Nordkette range. Seat of the Tyrolean princes from the 15th century, it was a favoured residence of Emperor Maximilian I, whose Golden Roof and cenotaph in the Court Church are landmarks. A university town, it hosted the Winter Olympics in 1964 and 1976.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/innsbruck.jpg" alt="Innsbruck" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Legner (simon04) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C3%BCrgerstra%C3%9Fe_26%2B24_(BT0A2830).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Innsbruck stands on the River Inn at its junction with the Wipp Valley, which gives access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a> 30 kilometres to the south. It lies in a broad valley between high mountains, the Karwendel Alps to the north and the Patscherkofel and the Serles to the south. The name means bridge over the Inn, and the town began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as a strategic crossing of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 4th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> set up an army station, Veldidena, at Oenipons, as Innsbruck was then called, to guard the commercial road from Verona over the Brenner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in their province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. The name Veldidena survives in the district of Wilten. In 1363 Innsbruck came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> control, and in 1429 it became the capital of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>.</p><p>In the 1490s Emperor Maximilian I also resided in the city, and it became a centre of European politics and culture. A regular postal service between Innsbruck and Mechelen was set up in 1490. The Golden Roof was completed around 1500, and the Court Church holds a funeral monument for Maximilian, partly erected by his successors, with a cenotaph and bronze statues of real and mythical ancestors of the emperor. After the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> the city was for a time part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was annexed by Nazi Germany in 1938. Innsbruck held two subcamps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau concentration camp</a>, and between 1943 and April 1945 it suffered twenty-two air raids and heavy damage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Innsbruck is the capital of the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the fifth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with 132,188 inhabitants. It hosted the Winter Olympics in 1964 and 1976 and the Winter Paralympics in 1984 and 1988. Among its modern buildings is the Bergisel ski jump, designed by Zaha Hadid and completed in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Innsbruck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Silver mining at Schwaz made Tyrol rich, and Maximilian I ruled from <b>Innsbruck</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
